A1. For butter or for worse (abbr.). ~ OLEO
A4. Something a flapper isn't when she stays home with her parents. ~ ALONE
A7. Anything that belongs to the "old man." ~ DADS
A10. Places reserved for confirmed yodlers. ~ ALPS
A12. The first man to be ordered to "move on!" ~ ADAM
A14. A side-door Pullman tourist. ~ BO
A16. What Nurmi runs around with. ~ EASE
A18. A persistent inebriate. ~ STEW
A19. What the ancient Egyptians shouted three times when the sun god made a touchdown. ~ RA
A20. What a lamp-post does when 18 horizontal looks at it. ~ SWAYS
A22. This comes from a contented cow. ~ MOO
A24. More recent. ~ NEWER
A26. A couple of printer's measures. ~ EMS
A27. The gantlet run by grooms when the knot is tied in a church. ~ AISLE
A29. When the wages of this are paid some people will get time and a half for overtime. ~ SIN
A30. There's much of this about nothing. ~ ADO
A31. Something Elks have. (Nothing to do with clubhouses or Fords.) ~ ANTLERS
A33. The cry of a schooner sinking on a bar. ~ SOS
A35. You can sing this in any flat if you have the right key. ~ SOLO
A37. On top of. ~ ATOP
A39. Correct. ~ OK
A40. Flapper's favorite fruit. ~ DATE
A41. The only place this Russian ruler means anything is in a crossword puzzle. ~ TSAR
A42. A finishing touch. (Ask any prize fighter.) ~ KO
A43. Abbreviation for a kind of college. ~ TECH
A45. Men look well with these on windy days. ~ EYES
A47. An overgrown marble. ~ TAW
A49. A lot of people are crazy about these fellows. ~ KEEPERS
A52. This makes the mare go, (We just put that in for a stall!) ~ HAY
A54. This comes from nurseries, Ford garages and cabarets. ~ DIN
A56. These get all broken up in the summer. ~ ROADS
A57. Spelled correctly this means something all women carry; spelled backwards it means something all women do. ~ BAG
A58. An Indian race. ~ AZTEC
A60. A point of the compass. (That's a big help.) ~ NNE
A61. A lobby. ~ FOYER
A63. Part of "to be." ~ BE
A64. What a beggar often does when he's hungry. ~ EATS
A66. The name of an Irish maid. ~ NORA
A68. The first note in music. ~ DO
A69. Girl's name. (That's another big help!) ~ EDNA
A71. There used to be one of these wherever people crossed a river; now there is one wherever they try to cross a street. ~ FORD
A73. These are things that people get caught in. ~ LIES
A74. When people see one of these it knocks them for a ghoul. ~ GHOST
A75. Futile interjection used on back fence soloists. ~ SCAT

D1. The eyes have it (pl.). ~ ORBS
D2. Each (abbr.). ~ EA
D3. Anything that belongs to Oley. ~ OLEYS
D4. A man who writes love letters. ~ ASS
D5. "Either's" affinity. ~ OR
D6. Something hungry Hungarians do. ~ EAT
D7. Late associate of Helen Maria. ~ DAWES
D8. Southern for "is." ~ AM
D9. What every movie actor wants to be. (Not divorced.) ~ STAR
D11. A French "step." ~ PAS
D13. Place for husbands to growl. ~ DEN
D15. What nearly all money is. ~ OWED
D17. Something saloons used to do to bums. ~ EMIT
D18. A shoe leather fish. ~ SOLE
D19. A famous parting place. ~ RENO
D21. Man's name. ~ AMOS
D23. Norwegian Capitol renamed for the convenience of crossword puzzle makers. ~ OSLO
D25. A hank of hair or anything else. ~ WISP
D27. What men take after they have a drink. ~ ANOTHER
D28. Things optimists and egotists don't use when they work crossword puzzles. ~ ERASERS
D30. Where all good sailors go. ~ ALOFT
D31. This goes with a lass. ~ ALACK
D32. What every dumb guest does when you want to go to bed. ~ STAYS
D34. Something a husband tells his bitter half when he comes home at 3 A.M. ~ STORY
D36. A short poem. (The shorter the better.) ~ ODE
D38. What gold-diggers dig for. ~ ORE
D43. Taunt. ~ TWIT
D44. A pair of horses. ~ SPAN
D46. An intoxicated "say." ~ SHAY
D48. A tool. ~ ADZE
D50. Ages. ~ EONS
D51. A famous garden advertised by William Jennings Bryan. ~ EDEN
D53. What messenger boys get if they run enough errands and live long enough. ~ AGED
D55. Wants. ~ NEEDS
D57. Untrained pigs. ~ BOARS
D58. Something Eve raised beside Cain. ~ ABEL
D59. What sardines come from. ~ CAN
D61. Where a swing goes after it goes to. ~ FRO
D62. Something pigs and baseball fans do. ~ ROOT
D65. A playful game. (Now you chase me!) ~ TAG
D67. When poetic Detroiters go to Canada. ~ OFT
D69. Electrical Engineers. (Backward abbr.). ~ EE
D70. The show me State (abbr.). ~ MO
D72. A district noted for its oil (abbr.). ~ DC


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
D1: The eyes have it (pl.). → ORBS
D23: Norwegian Capitol renamed for the convenience of crossword puzzle makers. → Capital

